---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import ContentSection from '../../components/ContentSection.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import LinkCard from '../../components/LinkCard.astro';

const relatedPages = [
  { title: 'All Tools', href: '/tools', description: 'Betting calculators hub' },
  { title: 'How Odds Work', href: '/how-betting-odds-work', description: 'Understanding odds formats' },
  { title: 'Common Mistakes', href: '/common-betting-mistakes', description: 'Avoiding betting errors' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Parlay Calculator"
  description="Understand parlay calculations, when parlays make sense, when they don't, and why the math usually favors the sportsbook."
>
  <Hero
    title="Parlay Calculator"
    subtitle="Understanding Combined Bet Payouts and Their True Cost"
    size="medium"
  />

  <div class="container content-container">
    <article class="page-content">
      <ContentSection>
        <div class="quick-take">
          <h2>Quick Take</h2>
          <p>
            A parlay combines multiple bets into one wager where all selections must win for the bet to pay out. While parlays offer larger potential returns, they're high-margin bets for sportsbooks because the vig compounds with each leg. This page explains how parlay odds work, when parlays might be appropriate, and why they're often a poor use of betting capital.
          </p>
        </div>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What Does This Tool Do?" id="what-does">
        <p>
          A parlay calculator converts the individual odds of each selection into a combined payout. It shows you:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>The total potential payout if all legs win</li>
          <li>The implied probability of the entire parlay hitting</li>
          <li>How the vig compounds across multiple legs</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The calculation itself is straightforward: convert each leg to decimal odds, multiply them together, and convert back to your preferred format. The tool handles this instantly.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="When Should I Use a Parlay Calculator?" id="when-use">
        <h3>Before placing any parlay</h3>
        <p>
          Always calculate the full payout and implied probability before betting. Parlays can look attractive because of big potential returns, but seeing the actual probability of winning helps ground your expectations.
        </p>

        <h3>When comparing parlay vs straight bets</h3>
        <p>
          Sometimes the question is whether to parlay two picks or bet them separately. The calculator helps you see the difference in expected value between these approaches.
        </p>

        <h3>When evaluating parlay promotions</h3>
        <p>
          Sportsbooks offer parlay boosts and profit increases. A calculator helps you determine whether the promotion actually creates value or just makes a bad bet look better.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="When Should I NOT Use a Parlay Calculator?" id="when-not-use">
        <Callout type="warning" title="Calculators Don't Make Parlays Smart">
          <p>
            Using a parlay calculator doesn't justify making parlays. It just tells you the math. If the underlying bets aren't +EV, combining them into a parlay doesn't create value—it destroys it faster.
          </p>
        </Callout>

        <h3>As justification for gambling</h3>
        <p>
          Seeing a potential $1,000 payout on a $10 bet is exciting but misleading. The calculator shows possibility, not probability. Don't use it to talk yourself into bets you know are long shots.
        </p>

        <h3>Without understanding the underlying math</h3>
        <p>
          If you don't understand why parlays compound the vig, a calculator won't help you make better decisions. Learn the concepts first.
        </p>

        <h3>For daily entertainment parlays</h3>
        <p>
          If you're parlaying every day for entertainment, the math is working against you aggressively. The calculator will just confirm how much you're giving up to the house.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Example Scenarios" id="examples">
        <h3>The "small bet, big win" appeal</h3>
        <p>
          A bettor wants to turn $20 into $500 with a 5-leg parlay of -110 favorites. The calculator shows the payout is around 25:1, but the implied probability of hitting all 5 legs is only about 3%. The bettor needs to decide: is this entertainment worth the expected loss, or would the $20 be better used on a single well-researched bet?
        </p>

        <h3>The correlated parlay</h3>
        <p>
          Some parlays combine outcomes that naturally relate (like a team winning and the game going under because they have a strong defense). Correlated parlays can occasionally offer value, but most sportsbooks restrict or price them accordingly. The calculator shows the standard payout; detecting correlation requires analysis.
        </p>

        <h3>The "sure thing" parlay</h3>
        <p>
          Heavy favorites parlayed together seem safe. Three -300 favorites combined into a parlay. The calculator reveals the combined probability of all three hitting is only about 42%—far from a sure thing. And the payout doesn't compensate for that risk.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What This Means for Bettors" id="what-means">
        <p>
          Parlay calculators reveal what parlays actually are:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>High-margin bets:</strong> The vig on each leg compounds, making parlays significantly more profitable for sportsbooks than straight bets.</li>
          <li><strong>Low-probability events:</strong> Even "likely" outcomes combined become unlikely quickly. Four 60% propositions parlayed have only a 13% combined chance of hitting.</li>
          <li><strong>Entertainment, not strategy:</strong> For most bettors, parlays should be treated as occasional entertainment with money you're comfortable losing, not as a path to profits.</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Common Misconceptions" id="misconceptions">
        <h3>"Parlays are the only way to win big"</h3>
        <p>
          Disciplined betting with good bankroll management can build profits over time. Parlays can create windfall moments, but they're not a sustainable approach.
        </p>

        <h3>"I just need to hit one"</h3>
        <p>
          The math doesn't work this way. If you're consistently playing -EV parlays, "hitting one" doesn't cover all the losses. You need to win at a rate higher than the implied odds to profit.
        </p>

        <h3>"Parlays are how sharps make money"</h3>
        <p>
          Most professional bettors avoid parlays or use them sparingly for specific correlated situations. High-volume straight bets with small edges are more common among successful bettors.
        </p>

        <h3>"Same-game parlays are different"</h3>
        <p>
          Same-game parlays (SGPs) combine outcomes within a single game. While they can include correlated legs, sportsbooks price them to maintain their edge. The calculator can't detect whether an SGP is fairly priced—that requires analysis.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="When This Matters Most" id="when-matters">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Before any multi-leg bet:</strong> Always calculate before placing parlays</li>
          <li><strong>When tempted by potential payouts:</strong> Use the calculator to see the reality behind the fantasy</li>
          <li><strong>When evaluating promotions:</strong> Parlay boosts need to overcome significant mathematical disadvantages</li>
          <li><strong>When comparing betting approaches:</strong> Straight bets vs parlays for the same picks</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Key Takeaways" id="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>Parlay calculators show payouts and combined probabilities</li>
          <li>The vig compounds with each leg, making parlays high-margin for sportsbooks</li>
          <li>Use the calculator before any parlay to understand what you're actually betting on</li>
          <li>Don't use exciting potential payouts to justify bad bets</li>
          <li>For most bettors, parlays should be entertainment, not strategy</li>
          <li>Understanding why parlays are structured this way matters more than the calculation itself</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Related Resources" id="related">
        <div class="related-grid">
          {relatedPages.map((page) => (
            <LinkCard
              title={page.title}
              href={page.href}
              description={page.description}
            />
          ))}
        </div>
      </ContentSection>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-content {
    padding: var(--spacing-3xl) 0;
  }

  .quick-take {
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent);
    padding: var(--spacing-lg);
    border-radius: 0 var(--border-radius) var(--border-radius) 0;
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xl);
  }

  .quick-take h2 {
    font-size: 1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm);
  }

  .quick-take p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  .related-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: var(--spacing-md);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-lg);
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .related-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
